WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.909 --> 00:00:04.889
So the following video you're about to see is a talk I gave at the

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:13.769
district of the South's Annual conference that's a part of the Orthodox Church of America, and so

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:17.458
the reason why I'm making this introduction is because for those of you

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:23.160
who -- a lot of the videos that I've been making I've been making a big effort to speak with words that are

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:27.239
understandable to everyone, but in this particular talk

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:33.660
I was surrounded by priests and the Archbishop of the South, and so

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:37.739
possibly some of the words that are being used

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:44.430
will be difficult for people who are coming from the outside to understand, and so what I've done is I'm going to put a glossary

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.669
as close enough to a glossary of words in

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:51.509
the description for a few basic things, and

00:00:53.320 --> 00:01:01.289
then I'm also going to try to add more images into the presentation so that it's as clear as possible for those who

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:08.070
who are chancing upon this. Oh, a last thing is that I've been receiving a lot of messages from people

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.960
who question whether or not the types of things I'm talking about

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:16.889
whether or not they're in line

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:21.750
let's say, with what actual Christians believe. A lot of people have been telling me that

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:28.829
that my version of Christianity is really nice, but that, you know, that's not what people in the church believe, and so I

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:36.420
Considering that, I would like you to ponder that I was surrounded by at least, what is it, 100

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:43.199
150 clergy, plus their wives and their families and the Archbishop of the South, and

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:52.280
although I'm sure some people didn't like my talk, no one was freaked out about what I was saying, and no one was

00:01:53.720 --> 00:02:02.760
acting as if I was speaking completely against the tradition. So, having said all that, I present to you

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.220
Pentecost for the Zombie Apocalypse

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:11.080
Your Eminence and reverend fathers, I want to thank you for this opportunity

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.270
 

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:15.070
to speak to you

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.070
When I gave the title of my talk

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:20.279
I didn't know I would be speaking during the dinner,

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:28.889
and so it's just a rather awkward time to talk about zombies, so

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:36.059
so I decided I was going to spare you the grisly details, and

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:40.260
so I've chosen images that are

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:44.070
cartoons and from children's images, and so actually, although

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:49.170
they're meant to be whimsical and funny, they might be more frightening because that's what they're trying to be.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.460
So, we live in a time of monsters.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:04.640
I announce this to you, but I think all of you already know that.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.520
We only need to glance at Popular Culture to realize this.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:17.520
At the narrative level of our culture and our stories, we've become obsessed with monsters;

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.880
with the strange, aliens,

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.180
with the marginal, with the glorification of the exception,

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.840
of things that don't fit.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.520
Most of us here probably grew up with

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:35.280
Sesame Street where we were told that "monsters are our friends"

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.920
and it's no longer in fiction by now

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.360
The Monsters

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:50.309
have left the dark spaces under our beds. They have left our nightmares to come out into the open.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.100
At a social level we can feel and see all around us the growing polarization,

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.660
the acceleration of what we can only call the

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.140
the breakdown, the decomposition of culture,

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:13.940
a progressive dissipation of the center which rallies us as a society

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.540
and in thinking about this it's difficult not to

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.160
to reminisce - it's difficult not to

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.120
to meditate on the famous poem by Yeats

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.500
"The Second Coming"

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:38.100
And we tend to go back to that poem because it seems, at least in our more frightening moments,

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:42.900
we get the feeling that the horrific cycle of the 20th century is

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:47.220
on our horizon again.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.640
Turning and turning in the widening gyre

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.100
The falcon cannot hear the falconer;

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.560
Things fall apart; the Center cannot hold

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.143
Mere anarchy is loosed upon the world

00:05:01.143 --> 00:05:03.130
The blood-dimmed tide is loosed

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:07.220
and everywhere the ceremony of innocence is drowned

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:09.380
The best lack all conviction

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.400
While the worst are full of passionate intensity

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:18.120
This poem of which I've quoted the first part

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.560
Is probably one of the most known in modern poetry, the most cited; I think it's actually the most

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.780
anthologized poem there is

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:33.100
And it rings so true because we stand here in that widening, widening gyre

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:38.720
At the Edge of the world where the wheel is spinning so fast

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.620
it feels as if it's going to come off its axis

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:50.680
And it is here on the edge that we find the zombies

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:55.400
wandering aimlessly in a world that's losing its center

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:05.880
Unlike most of his monstrous brothers, let's say, the zombie is truly the harbinger of contemporary Nihilism.

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:09.700
The zombie has no magic.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.820
The arrival of the zombie usually has no clear reason.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:21.100
But rather, the zombie is couched in a biological accident: a disease, a plague.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.340
It's simply an animated corpse.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:29.080
The zombie inhabits the indeterminate space of living death.

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:35.060
Roaming around in packs, the zombie shows us the mindless wandering of the mindless mob

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.040
with an insatiable hunger for devouring others,

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.500
for swallowing life.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.500
If the vampire is the monster of aristocracy

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:50.440
the zombie is the monster of the mass, of the demos;

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:55.560
the monster of the accidental, of quantitative leveling.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:58.460
The zombie is the atheist insistent

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:01.060
on the illusion of free will.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:07.920
It's an image of nihilism and an idiosyncrasy taken all the way to decomposition

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:15.300
In every major city in North America, they have these events

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:18.920
they call them "Zombie Walks"

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.880
and people dress up as zombies and walk in the thousands

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.020
down the streets dressed up and made up to look like corpses,

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:33.180
shifting around with dead empty eyes, pretending to be the Walking Dead.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.920
Some of these are huge.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:42.200
if we were generous

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:45.680
we could say that it is a zombie procession

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.600
We could call it

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:53.220
You could say that they're liturgical zombies, if there's such a thing.

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:03.240
The Zombie both typifies the Mob while simultaneously the absolute individualism,

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:08.040
the absolute isolation of contemporary life.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:15.020
The zombies in a horde only interact with what they desire, and they never interact with each other.

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:24.460
And the trope of cannibalism is very ancient. It's there in all the ancient stories, all the ancient myths,

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:30.700
but the tweak, in zombies, on that theme:

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:34.360
of desiring to eat the brain

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:36.980
It's a very powerful one

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:43.880
Because it's truly the image of the nihilist, because the zombie is a creature without meaning, without intelligence.

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:48.380
It misses any form of personhood.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:54.285
And it has this insatiable desire which mirrors what it lacks;

00:08:54.285 --> 00:08:58.740
it desires what it lacks: identity, meaning.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:02.080
And this desire appears in that materialist reduction

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:07.140
of identity and personhood to that clump of cells that we have up in our cranium.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:15.100
The zombie wants to eat your brains, because it cannot eat the mind, and it cannot inhabit the mind.

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:21.580
But strangely enough, this desire to eat the living is the extreme

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:24.460
perversion of our desire for communion.

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:28.840
And it's also a distilled image of all our passions,

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:32.440
our attempt to fill the unquenchable yearning

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:40.040
through our passion, which always transforms other people into commodities that we think will bring us what we need.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:46.740
So the zombie is both an image of the social breakdown

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:49.640
the person as a meaningless statistic,

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:55.420
the disappearance of common values, except the overwhelming desire to consume.

00:09:56.580 --> 00:10:04.640
But so it is also the breakdown of the person itself into a soulless desiring death machine.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:09.280
There's a strict analogy between these different levels of the world:

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:18.740
the social breakdown and polarization is to the State what the abandonment to the passions is for the Person.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:25.640
And the zombie is both of those fragmentations at the same time.

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:30.660
So as we pull back a bit, as we look around,

00:10:31.059 --> 00:10:37.229
as the narrative fabric of our world begins to fill in quantities that are barely possible to believe

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.520
with images of the monstrous

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:45.800
as we feel the world being torn apart by fragmentation and conflict

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:49.640
We are simultaneously as individuals being constantly

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.600
assaulted by images

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:56.740
images with the purpose of awakening our own desires.

00:10:57.819 --> 00:11:02.369
And we've come to the point where we - most of us - so many of us have been accustomed

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:08.040
to the constant exposure to the stranger and stranger fringe of desire

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:13.300
while being enticed by the siren song to indulge,

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:19.100
to give in to the waves and the storm and to sink into the mire.

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:27.540
Now as we here, hopefully, who still hold on to a liturgical world

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:32.800
not the zombie kind, the divine kind

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:36.900
a world which strives out of pattern and purpose

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:45.480
We can both stand in a church where Christ, in the dome, is the origin of the heavenly hierarchy of beings

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:51.600
and we also know that we can find hesychia in our heart, in our center

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:54.636
as a gateway to the Divine,

00:11:54.636 --> 00:12:00.980
and as the manager of the passions whirling in the widening gyre.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:11.240
Just like our individual unholy unions with death appear on the edge of our beings

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:18.020
where, through an excessive attachment to our senses, we mingle with our passions and produce our own aberrations,

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:21.900
the Cosmic Monster lies on the Edge of the World,

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:25.200
the Edge and the End of the World

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:28.880
Those two things: the Edge and the End,

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:35.420
they represent the same structure. They're the same thing in our stories

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:43.100
and so even when he is a zombie, Man is the Microcosm.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:51.000
The notion that a movement towards the Edge and a movement

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:56.800
towards the End have similar natures is one which is right there in the beginning of Scripture.

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:00.100
When Adam and Eve fall

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:03.240
they are told that they will die --

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:06.080
but that's not immediately what happens is it?

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:11.109
In fact, what happens is they move away from the central tree

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:16.060
past the Walls of the Garden, and out into the wilderness.

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:25.820
And similarly Adam, as he moves out from the center into history,

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:29.280
Adam and Eve are given garments of skin (Gen 3:21):

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:37.140
an outer layer of animality, an outer layer of death to protect them from death.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:41.920
If our mind is struggling to

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:46.780
get a sense of what that means, it's very difficult to understand what that means

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.240
that we were given a protection of death

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:52.440
a layer of death to protect us from death.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:57.920
I always think of a vaccine. It's probably the best image for that.

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:06.760
The garments of skin are seen by the Church Fathers as all that is added to our nature in the world of the fall,

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:09.480
the layers of society, of technology

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:12.640
and those are the flipside, and in a way,

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:19.119
they're of the same nature as our weakness, and the passions which come from the world of death.

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:24.760
St. Gregory of Nyssa tells us that the hairs on our body are a symbol of death.

00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:31.540
They're without feeling and dead and appear as our spatial extremities,

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:34.340
the very limit of our garments of skin,

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:41.080
just as death appears as our temporal extremity of this earthly life.

00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:46.740
The giants in the Bible,

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:50.110
those that appeared before the Flood

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:53.700
with a sign of confusion and mixture

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:57.680
wrapped in inappropriate sexual desire -

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:06.940
in the tradition of Enoch, they are seen as the origin of technology and magic, and they were known to create hybrids.

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:10.490
They're full of excess

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:18.760
reveling in conflict until absolute chaos could no longer be held at bay, and the world was undone in the waters

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:23.200
"The ceremony of Innocence was drowned"

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:28.400
in sophisticated hybrid non-binary fluid categories.

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:38.020
And in a very parallel way the ancients described the world geographically as a giant Island

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:40.549
surrounded by a vast ocean

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:44.329
whose fringes were inhabited by Impossible Monsters,

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:48.440
these signs of the breakdown and the confusion,

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:52.729
the confusion of categories, which is death

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:57.319
basically, the cosmic garments of skin.

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:04.100
These images that I'm showing you are based on the descriptions of the faraway races by

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:10.400
the first century Pliny the Elder, who died during the Vesuvius eruption

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:13.060
which destroyed Pompeii.

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:21.360
So now imagine how Pliny the Elder would have described these faraway specimens:

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:27.800
But I need to be careful because as we stand here possibly

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:31.220
in that widening gyre,

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:35.300
possibly nearing the teetering edge of the world,

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:40.600
it would be too easy to point and say, "There they are," right? "There they are,

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:44.380
the degenerate ones. There they are, the monsters, and thank God

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:48.740
we're safe in the church." And like I mentioned and like I'll be saying all through this talk:

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:55.500
the same chaos which lies on the Edge of the World also lies on the Edge of each one of us

00:16:57.900 --> 00:17:03.740
But I think it's still possible though to look around and to read the signs of the time.

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:10.660
So how do we make sense of this? As we look around us and find the flood waters rising,

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:14.440
how do we navigate through the growing chaos?

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:17.660
In the back of my mind

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:20.760
there's a saying which plays over and over

00:17:22.439 --> 00:17:28.278
maybe in the hopes of convincing myself, and it is the last promise made by Christ before his Ascension:

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:32.920
"Behold I am with you until the end of the age" (Mt 28:20)

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:36.580
And we know as Orthodox Christians that this promise

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:41.480
also implies that Christ is with us until the limits of the world,

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:49.850
with us until the bottom of the world, the bottom of death where Christ crushed the head of the serpent as

00:17:50.039 --> 00:17:52.039
he descended into Hades.

00:17:56.549 --> 00:18:01.819
The promise Christ made when he was about to ascend to the Father, the promise of Pentecost.

00:18:03.419 --> 00:18:08.989
It echoes the promise of the Spirit that he made in the Gospel of St. John Chapter 14.

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:14.509
Pentecost is the possibility of

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:17.240
moving in Christ,

00:18:17.549 --> 00:18:19.668
from the Upper Room,

00:18:20.399 --> 00:18:22.399
from the top,

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:29.059
from the highest place, or from the center, let's say, no matter how you frame it, but to move in Christ until the

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:33.000
End of the World, until the End of the age.

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:39.240
It is the Church ablaze, a fire that spreads and consumes until the age itself.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:46.240
It consumes, but like the fire that Moses saw on Sinai, the Church is not consumed.

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:57.780
The icon of Pentecost is a cosmic image

00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:10.260
It shows us a type of multiplicity, which is not fragmentation,

00:19:10.780 --> 00:19:14.940
a type of multiplicity which is not the decomposition of our times

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:18.900
but is a multiplication which does not forget its center.

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:22.339
In the terms of the Philokalia

00:19:22.339 --> 00:19:27.540
as we move into multiplicity, Pentecost is the possibility of the remembrance of God,

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:30.760
the remembrance of God even unto death.

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:35.460
If we look at the image, we see the icons are seated in an arch,

00:19:36.279 --> 00:19:38.518
which is a proxy of the church itself

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:42.780
The icon of Pentecost is one of those icons which,

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:49.620
although it does represent an event in the Bible, it represents it in a manner that goes beyond the event and

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:54.320
becomes a permanent image of how the Church exists in the world

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:59.580
and the hint that this is the case is that although St. Paul was not there at the

00:20:00.099 --> 00:20:05.369
event of the Bible, he is represented in this icon and here, St. Peter and

00:20:05.369 --> 00:20:09.760
St. Paul are represented together as the pillars of the Church

00:20:10.060 --> 00:20:12.740
as the left hand and right hand of Christ

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:17.720
Above the Apostles is the Holy Fire which descends on them

00:20:18.309 --> 00:20:23.098
This fire separates into twelve, just as the Apostles themselves each acquired

00:20:23.679 --> 00:20:25.679
tongues of fire

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:29.500
tongues which speak in a manner that can reveal Christ through

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:33.020
multiplicity and be heard by all men.

00:20:33.789 --> 00:20:39.508
And then, down in the bottom of the icon is the door which leads outside the Upper Room,

00:20:39.729 --> 00:20:42.629
it is the door which leads out into the outer darkness

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:53.640
And in the door is represented the allegorical figure of the Cosmos itself, which holds the scrolls, the fully manifest

00:20:54.309 --> 00:21:01.468
form of the twelve traditions issued by the one fire, the one Church whose unity is preserved in its multiplicity

00:21:03.630 --> 00:21:07.979
And, of course, the door of Pentecost is the door of the Church itself,

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:10.889
the Church considered at all levels of

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:18.900
interpretation that we can understand that statement. It's both the door of the Church in the sense of the cosmic body of Christ

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:25.049
but it's also the door on the building of your local church, the door which leads out of the nave, out of the narthex

00:21:25.419 --> 00:21:27.419
into the chaotic outside.

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:32.070
Now in earlier traditions of Pentecost

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:39.750
what was represented in the door was not the synthetic image of Cosmos, and the very earliest images

00:21:40.330 --> 00:21:46.919
that I could find in this form of Pentecost - they all come from right after Iconoclasm and

00:21:47.350 --> 00:21:49.350
they represent an empty door

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:55.079
but very soon in the door was represented a crowd of foreigners,

00:21:56.380 --> 00:22:01.289
all with different hats and different clothing  and different shades of skin color.

00:22:02.919 --> 00:22:06.299
This is one of my favorite versions:

00:22:06.299 --> 00:22:12.839
It's from a Serbian monastery, and here the door of Pentecost is actually a window

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:16.559
which leads outside of the church,

00:22:18.159 --> 00:22:23.519
and the foreigners appear coming towards the Church from the edge, on the sides.

00:22:28.150 --> 00:22:35.729
There's a tradition from Armenia which portrays a monster in the door of Pentecost.

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:41.429
The monster is either a dog-headed man,

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:44.140
a cynocephalus,

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:46.140
or else it is truly

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:50.020
an unnameable monster, a kind of chaotic being

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:53.560
with a dog-face growing behind its head.

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:59.000
This was the image that I chose as the first slide of my presentation.

00:23:00.130 --> 00:23:06.630
Now the first time I saw this image, I came across it in a book and I was startled

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:09.090
beyond belief

00:23:09.309 --> 00:23:11.309
I mean, I guess you can imagine.

00:23:13.059 --> 00:23:16.859
But as I began to understand the image of Pentecost

00:23:17.230 --> 00:23:24.360
I realized to what extent this is a testimony to the totality of what Christianity is meant to be,

00:23:26.049 --> 00:23:29.668
to the true possibility of God being all in all.

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:41.429
The monster, as an inhabitant of the margin, is a token of that which does not fit

00:23:42.190 --> 00:23:44.909
within what we know, cannot be measured in the

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:53.060
categories that we are aware of, it appears as a freak, as the ultimate Unexpected.

00:23:54.280 --> 00:24:03.080
So, the monster is both the encounter with the surprise of the future as we move forward in time

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:09.000
(so to encounter the unexpected), but the monster is also the encounter with the surprise of the Edge,

00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:16.080
as the Church expands into the dark corners of the world, but also the dark corners of our own lives.

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:20.840
As the Spirit of God transforms us, for most of us there will always be

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:24.640
foreign elements, monsters hiding in our garments of skin.

00:24:26.890 --> 00:24:31.829
So here, in Pentecost, this monster is represented as not yet in the Church,

00:24:34.540 --> 00:24:39.480
but as part of the potential into which the Church can grow,

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:43.120
as part of that which can hear the gospel in its language

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:52.060
Now I want to issue -- before I keep going -- I want to issue a caveat if there are some

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:58.680
cryptozoologists in the room, or like conspiracy types who talk about Lizard Men who live under the earth

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:03.360
Like, you don't have to identify yourself, but this next part is for you.

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:08.840
We have to be extremely careful when we read ancient accounts.

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:15.900
We don't want to interpret them in rather materialistic categories of genus and species.

00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:20.280
In the Bible, a bird is an animal with wings. So a bat is a bird.

00:25:20.919 --> 00:25:25.018
And a fish is something in the water, so whales and dolphins are fish.

00:25:26.230 --> 00:25:33.149
And so we don't have to imagine some kind of cynocephalic people out there that would have some genetic

00:25:33.639 --> 00:25:35.169
relationship to dogs

00:25:35.169 --> 00:25:43.169
anymore than we used to think that a hippo-potamus, or a "river horse," need have a genetic relationship with horses.

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:47.519
Just as the ancient called those they could not understand

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:51.299
"barbarians," that is, those who bark like dogs,

00:25:52.269 --> 00:25:56.039
The notion of dog-headed man appears as this encounter

00:25:57.120 --> 00:26:01.840
with an aspect of humanity which we can barely see because they're

00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:05.260
so far removed from our capacity to properly see them.

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:12.800
If I hear someone speaking Cantonese, I am not going to understand - I will hear chaos.

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:21.060
And it's difficult to have that experience now because we've seen everything. But if you just maybe imagine seeing

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:25.620
an Australian Bushman 500 years ago,

00:26:26.169 --> 00:26:29.008
it is possible to see chaos.

00:26:31.899 --> 00:26:36.419
But the notion of the cynocephalus has an important history in Christianity.

00:26:37.629 --> 00:26:39.629
It comes, of course, from the

00:26:40.269 --> 00:26:42.628
Hellenic past, from the legends of Alexander the Great.

00:26:43.240 --> 00:26:48.539
But the image of the dog-headed man became the central image of the Christian Edge of the world,

00:26:49.179 --> 00:26:51.359
the ultimate hostile Foreigner.

00:26:54.100 --> 00:26:59.519
Personally, I think that it partly became so important because of its strong analogies in the Bible.

00:27:00.519 --> 00:27:02.789
Christ speaks to the Canaanite woman

00:27:04.029 --> 00:27:09.839
and he tells her that one should not give to dogs the bread that is meant for the children (Mt 15:26)

00:27:10.419 --> 00:27:12.749
and the answer of the Canaanite woman

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:16.919
is analogous to what we see in this icon,

00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:23.309
that "even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table."

00:27:24.070 --> 00:27:28.379
So here in the icon of Pentecost, we see this dog not so much at the end of the table

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:31.740
but maybe on the Edge of the Church, maybe on the Edge of the World.

00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:37.480
This one is not a Canaanite,

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:40.480
but a Caninite.

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:42.960
That might seem like a really bad pun

00:27:43.810 --> 00:27:45.810
but I assure you that

00:27:47.080 --> 00:27:54.840
it will cause modern interpreters to cry foul in reading these ancient accounts, and will say that all of this

00:27:55.180 --> 00:28:00.300
question is just a matter of bad transcription.

00:28:02.440 --> 00:28:04.440
I don't think so.

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:07.860
All these foreigners in the icon of Pentecost,

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:13.460
culminating into the impossible monster, are the surprise we discover on the Edge of the Church

00:28:14.440 --> 00:28:20.120
anticipating, maybe waiting for the crumbs, maybe waiting for the antidoron (blessed bread),

00:28:20.180 --> 00:28:23.480
which we can imagine flowing out of the Church,

00:28:24.250 --> 00:28:30.960
so that even those we can barely recognize as our brothers can have a foretaste of the Kingdom of Heaven.

00:28:33.910 --> 00:28:37.649
Now the most famous dog-headed man

00:28:38.530 --> 00:28:40.530
some of you know where I'm going with this

00:28:42.190 --> 00:28:44.190
is, of course, St. Christopher.

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:50.249
St. Christopher's traditional icon represents him as a dog-headed warrior.

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:59.520
There are many who have tried to say that this tradition of St. Christopher as a dog-headed man

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:04.340
is late, and like I said, is just a misunderstanding of ancient texts, and

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:07.280
I don't know, it depends what you mean by "late."

00:29:08.530 --> 00:29:13.739
This is the first image of St. Christopher we have; it's from the 6th or the 7th century.

00:29:14.740 --> 00:29:20.070
So it's before iconoclasm, which means that we're lucky we even have this image

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:24.240
There are probably many more, but they've just been destroyed by the Iconoclasts.

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:31.000
It's a ceramic icon depicting him next to St. George, each killing snakes.

00:29:31.600 --> 00:29:34.680
And this is just one of my favorite images in the world.

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:42.600
So here's the the Monster and Monster Killer - two warriors facing the dragons.

00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:46.840
And it's funny how they they keep telling us that the legend of St. Christopher

00:29:46.840 --> 00:29:50.100
killing the dragon is from the 12th century or something.

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:57.100
It always seems like scholars are telling Christians that we're deluding ourselves

00:29:57.100 --> 00:29:59.820
and that a world cannot exist without a textual reference.

00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:03.000
But here it is.

00:30:05.560 --> 00:30:09.779
So there was an attempt to eliminate this icon in the Russian synodal period

00:30:10.180 --> 00:30:16.739
because it did not fit modern sensibilities, and I've had many people, especially priests, tell me that

00:30:16.740 --> 00:30:18.780
they're really bothered by this icon and

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:27.100
It's funny because, although I have tried to explain it, and in a certain manner, defend it.

00:30:27.100 --> 00:30:33.780
For example, I wrote an article about St. Christopher in the Orthodox Arts Journal, and it is one of our most popular articles.

00:30:34.330 --> 00:30:36.839
It has been viewed over 20,000 times.

00:30:38.050 --> 00:30:42.450
When an article about a dog-headed Saint is one of your most popular articles,

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:46.600
I would call that a sign of the times, people

00:30:50.020 --> 00:30:54.100
But having said that I would be a bit disturbed myself

00:30:54.520 --> 00:31:01.340
if we were not at least bothered by this icon -- I mean my goodness! it is a dog headed man, after all.

00:31:03.860 --> 00:31:06.720
And I think that, in a way, that that's the point.

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:10.660
I think that that's the point.

00:31:10.660 --> 00:31:16.920
I always like to say that St. Christopher, the icon of St. Christopher is the "Last Icon."

00:31:18.730 --> 00:31:25.290
It's on the very edge of iconography and because it is so, it will always appear as ambiguous

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:30.330
and straddle the border of what is acceptable or unacceptable in icons.

00:31:30.940 --> 00:31:37.409
In the Greek tradition, the icon of St. Christopher was actually placed above the exit door of the church

00:31:38.110 --> 00:31:40.500
so that it would be the last thing you saw

00:31:41.320 --> 00:31:43.320
going out into the world

00:31:44.289 --> 00:31:47.488
And in the west, St. Christopher was placed on bridges

00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:54.659
where he would represent the uneasy space of the transition, the in-between space.

00:31:59.169 --> 00:32:01.169
I think St. Christopher is

00:32:01.179 --> 00:32:04.228
crucially important to understand right now.

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:09.389
Basically, I think that St. Christopher is the Patron Saint of the End of the World.

00:32:11.289 --> 00:32:16.949
And although he's not my official patron, I would say that he's become so unofficially.

00:32:18.039 --> 00:32:20.608
Having lived abroad for many years

00:32:21.639 --> 00:32:28.139
and having traveled much I've witnessed many miracles and I've seen many signs surrounding his presence in my life.

00:32:30.220 --> 00:32:34.139
So this is one of the icons that hangs in my workshop.

00:32:35.889 --> 00:32:40.679
And also, I think that we'll need St. Christopher when the zombies come.

00:32:46.479 --> 00:32:53.699
So as we face the rising chaos, as we face the zombies, we recall  the words of the Psalmist,

00:32:54.039 --> 00:32:58.738
"I am surrounded by my enemies who are like lions hungry for human flesh" (Ps 57:4)

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:06.149
The immediate danger is that we become afraid, like St. Peter walking on the waters.

00:33:08.229 --> 00:33:10.229
There's a trope in zombie stories,

00:33:10.840 --> 00:33:16.949
according to which, no matter how bad the situation is, no matter how hard the zombies try to get in,

00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:21.540
the true danger lies within

00:33:21.540 --> 00:33:24.800
the true danger lies in how the human group

00:33:25.359 --> 00:33:31.769
shuts themselves off, and completely isolates themselves, and become pathological in their isolation.

00:33:34.749 --> 00:33:39.899
The fear of facing the possibility of the flood is to think that, as in the story of Noah,

00:33:40.809 --> 00:33:44.579
the door of the Church must be closed and sealed shut.

00:33:47.379 --> 00:33:53.549
Maybe we can get together and isolate ourselves completely. I mean, it's tempting isn't it?

00:33:54.429 --> 00:33:57.538
And we've heard some intimations of that tendency recently.

00:33:59.800 --> 00:34:04.889
But Christ promised us that the gates of hell will not prevail on the doors of the church

00:34:06.250 --> 00:34:12.390
And I'm not saying here there isn't a time to seal the door of the church. Obviously, that's what we do, at least symbolically,

00:34:12.970 --> 00:34:14.970
after the exit of the catechumens

00:34:15.600 --> 00:34:18.860
We should, and must, protect our Mysteries.

00:34:18.860 --> 00:34:22.460
In fact, it rather seems sometimes that we've been sheepish in doing so.

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:25.440
I want to show you something.

00:34:28.120 --> 00:34:33.240
Once I encountered an image of Pentecost from a medieval Coptic church.

00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:35.800
Can you see the difference?

00:34:37.620 --> 00:34:43.240
It was the first time I saw an icon of Pentecost where the door was closed and

00:34:44.590 --> 00:34:47.430
it disturbed me profoundly to see such an image.

00:34:47.430 --> 00:34:53.940
And it took time before I understood why such an image would even have come into existence

00:34:55.080 --> 00:35:01.100
I realized, at least I speculated, that the Copts had become so beset on all sides

00:35:01.740 --> 00:35:03.740
by their opponents,

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:07.920
had been so besieged in their dhimmi status (subjection under Islam),

00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:12.900
that it was inevitable that they represent the door of Pentecost as closed.

00:35:13.980 --> 00:35:16.460
It's food for thought.

00:35:17.260 --> 00:35:22.440
But to see such an image should make us weep and pray

00:35:22.860 --> 00:35:30.560
that even as we become besieged we find a way to keep the door of Pentecost open, for if we close those doors

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:34.180
we abandon the world to its fragmentation.

00:35:41.040 --> 00:35:44.320
I want to tell you the story of St. Christopher.

00:35:46.980 --> 00:35:48.780
You've all heard it.

00:35:48.780 --> 00:35:50.780
I want to tell it, maybe in a different way.

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:56.380
The version of the story which we know best actually comes from the western tradition.

00:35:56.600 --> 00:36:02.180
In the western tradition, St. Christopher is not a dog-headed man, but a giant.

00:36:03.280 --> 00:36:08.880
He's not a Caninite, but a Caananite,

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:11.820
and, in an important way,  he's a Cainite,

00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:19.160
that is a descendant of Cain who associated with the nephilim, the giants of the Genesis Flood.

00:36:20.310 --> 00:36:24.259
So although he's not a dog-headed man in the western tradition,

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:28.880
and some people have felt safe and represented this version of

00:36:28.920 --> 00:36:34.340
the icon in their churches because they didn't like the dog-headed version - and that's understandable -

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:41.620
But even though he's a Cainite, St. Christopher is still like the Canaanite woman,

00:36:41.620 --> 00:36:45.480
the "dog on the edge of the table" who asks Christ for the crumbs.

00:36:46.640 --> 00:36:50.520
So, as hard as you try, you will not get the dog out of St. Christopher

00:36:54.480 --> 00:36:58.600
These are a few hybrid icons, joining the western tradition with the eastern one.

00:37:02.040 --> 00:37:09.949
The story of St. Christopher has him as a vicious warrior in Canaan who is looking to serve the most powerful man in the world.

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:17.239
He found the most renowned and powerful king, until he sees that this king is afraid of the devil.

00:37:18.080 --> 00:37:20.760
So he goes to serve the devil.

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:28.360
And in this we can see St. Christopher going all the way to the end, all the way

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:34.999
to the edge of what is imaginable, in becoming a servant of the evil one directly.

00:37:44.540 --> 00:37:51.380
Then St. Christopher notices that the evil one is afraid of the Cross, so he decides he will serve Christ.

00:37:52.860 --> 00:38:00.780
And when the story, at least in the version that's in the Golden Legend, Saint Christopher is also a trickster

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:06.680
because he tells those he serves, for example, he informs the king that

00:38:06.930 --> 00:38:11.629
unless the king tells him who the devil is, that he will no longer serve that king.

00:38:12.500 --> 00:38:16.480
But in answering St. Christopher, this becomes the very

00:38:16.620 --> 00:38:22.740
reason why Saint Christopher stops serving the king, and then looks for the next most powerful

00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:25.860
person, which is the devil.

00:38:25.860 --> 00:38:31.560
And then, he does the same to the devil: he tricks the devil himself

00:38:31.700 --> 00:38:33.700
to reveal Christ to him.

00:38:36.140 --> 00:38:40.080
We need to pay attention to this detail, because

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:48.280
I think it's an image of what St. Christopher can be for us, the key to

00:38:48.860 --> 00:38:56.620
the double inversion, which transforms the Fall and Death into Resurrection, the "trampling of death by death."

00:38:58.980 --> 00:39:04.670
In his search for Christ he comes to a hermit in the desert who teaches him the way of Christianity.

00:39:05.180 --> 00:39:09.660
But when it comes to the practice, St. Christopher cannot follow the rule,

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:13.260
cannot follow the standard. I mean he is a monster, after all.

00:39:14.120 --> 00:39:17.660
The Hermit says to fast, to abstain.

00:39:18.980 --> 00:39:21.220
St. Christopher replies that he cannot.

00:39:22.200 --> 00:39:24.200
The Hermit says to pray.

00:39:25.260 --> 00:39:27.620
St. Christopher says he cannot.

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:33.530
So the hermit says that St. Christopher should stand next to the river and

00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:37.460
cross those who need to cross on his shoulders.

00:39:39.450 --> 00:39:41.450
I want you to notice how

00:39:42.180 --> 00:39:45.349
profound the story becomes here

00:39:46.770 --> 00:39:52.520
We often think of the Ark built by Noah as a holy thing and, in a certain way, it is.

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:59.280
We often imagine the nave, or even the whole church as a holy thing, and in many ways, it is.

00:39:59.880 --> 00:40:05.940
But in another very, very important way, the ark is a boat full of beasts.

00:40:09.100 --> 00:40:12.060
And the Church is a hospital full of the sick.

00:40:13.260 --> 00:40:18.920
So, since Christopher stands on the Edge of the world, he stands on the bank of the Flood,

00:40:22.080 --> 00:40:28.300
when the Logos the Christ Emmanuel, who is the Logos implicit, the seed of God in all things,

00:40:28.300 --> 00:40:29.560
comes to the edge,

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:36.420
well, it is the monster, like an Ark, who can carry him across.

00:40:38.460 --> 00:40:40.460
I want you to think of that hermit.

00:40:41.200 --> 00:40:44.240
You see, the hermit did not tell St. Christopher

00:40:44.920 --> 00:40:48.580
that it was fine that he could not follow fasting and prayer.

00:40:48.580 --> 00:40:51.660
I mean, he didn't say that, since St. Christopher

00:40:52.420 --> 00:40:58.640
thought that it was against his nature to fast and pray the hermit didn't tell him that the Church had been wrong all along.

00:40:58.640 --> 00:41:00.380
No

00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:03.000
But the hermit,

00:41:03.369 --> 00:41:07.940
in wisdom and in economía, he brought St. Christopher to the edge,

00:41:07.940 --> 00:41:13.340
he brought St. Christopher into the narthex, on the Edge of the Flood,

00:41:13.980 --> 00:41:18.860
where he could be the ambiguous figure he was in the first place,

00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:24.900
and eventually, and mysteriously, become a vehicle for his Lord.

00:41:24.900 --> 00:41:25.400
 

00:41:30.970 --> 00:41:36.540
There's a woman who comes to our parish. Her name is Margeline.

00:41:38.950 --> 00:41:40.950
Margeline is homeless,

00:41:41.860 --> 00:41:46.469
and if sometimes she's able to find a place to stay it never lasts long

00:41:47.950 --> 00:41:55.709
She's never able to find stability. There's something - some thrust - in her which makes it impossible to remain stable.

00:41:57.130 --> 00:42:02.039
Generous people from the parish have tried to help her find a place, to help her find some stability, but

00:42:04.060 --> 00:42:06.060
it never lasts long.

00:42:08.680 --> 00:42:11.720
Margeline is a hermaphrodite.

00:42:13.540 --> 00:42:16.720
Her appearance is confusing,

00:42:17.400 --> 00:42:24.380
as she straddles the limits of what we understand, straddles the limits of the normal categories

00:42:24.840 --> 00:42:26.840
that constitute the world.

00:42:29.560 --> 00:42:31.740
Margeline is paranoid,

00:42:31.960 --> 00:42:38.280
and possibly more: when you speak with her she jumps from one thing to the next, one subject to the next.

00:42:38.480 --> 00:42:41.400
Her conversation is disjointed and fragmented;

00:42:42.660 --> 00:42:47.880
to speak with her is to feel like she's constantly under attack.

00:42:50.040 --> 00:42:55.700
Margeline comes and sits in the narthex, and she usually stays there for the whole service.

00:42:58.210 --> 00:43:03.450
And very rarely, she gets up and she walks into the nave and she sits down in the nave,

00:43:04.120 --> 00:43:10.120
but she always stays just for a few minutes, and then she returns to the narthex.

00:43:11.940 --> 00:43:14.340
We've invited her to come in,

00:43:14.440 --> 00:43:21.160
our priest has spoken to her, asked her if she wanted to be catechized, if she wanted to join,

00:43:21.240 --> 00:43:23.880
and join into full communion,

00:43:25.540 --> 00:43:28.220
but Margeline returns to the narthex.

00:43:29.460 --> 00:43:36.480
She waits until coffee hour, she eats with us, and hopes that someone will give her some money

00:43:37.240 --> 00:43:39.240
before she leaves.

00:43:41.479 --> 00:43:44.889
Margeline has memorized the services.

00:43:46.309 --> 00:43:50.079
Sometimes she makes little comments about some detail,

00:43:50.869 --> 00:43:54.278
you know, some small error or confusion in the service.

00:43:54.859 --> 00:44:00.518
Maybe the choir was off a little bit. Maybe someone forgot some small detail.

00:44:01.060 --> 00:44:03.420
And she knows what's happening in the parish,

00:44:04.300 --> 00:44:09.700
the politics and the jostling which sometimes takes place in human affairs.

00:44:12.259 --> 00:44:16.988
And we hear strands of that, we hear these hyper-lucid

00:44:17.540 --> 00:44:23.860
comments - they appear as surprises - in her usual frenetic conversation.

00:44:26.380 --> 00:44:31.880
One Sunday, Margeline reached into her backpack.

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:36.300
She carries this backpack around with her, and she reached into her backpack and she took out

00:44:37.729 --> 00:44:43.179
these three small McDonald toys, you know, the kind of toy you get if you buy a Happy Meal.

00:44:43.940 --> 00:44:48.380
She took these three toys, and she gave them to my children.

00:44:51.040 --> 00:44:56.360
There's a tradition in Indian culture, according to which to receive a gift from a beggar

00:44:56.360 --> 00:44:58.820
is to receive a blessing from God.

00:45:00.620 --> 00:45:03.100
There are those who whisper in the parish, I don't know who,

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:10.360
but there's someone whispering in the parish that, maybe, Margeline is an angel.

00:45:17.440 --> 00:45:24.080
Once, in speaking to her, she told me that she also attends a mosque,

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:32.640
that there is a Muslim couple who will often pick her up on Friday so that she can participate in the prayer.

00:45:39.009 --> 00:45:41.009
So, I would ask all of you

00:45:42.309 --> 00:45:47.930
in that last sentence that I just added to that story, all of us to examine what our hearts did

00:45:47.930 --> 00:45:49.528
when I added that last part

00:45:50.079 --> 00:45:53.399
There are some of us here who might be too happy

00:45:54.339 --> 00:45:58.620
that I mentioned the detail about the mosque, and there are some here

00:45:58.620 --> 00:46:01.619
who might be too annoyed that I mentioned that detail.

00:46:03.009 --> 00:46:06.029
But Margeline just sits in the narthex, after all.

00:46:07.120 --> 00:46:10.820
There's something about the margin

00:46:11.600 --> 00:46:14.660
which always arouses our passions.

00:46:19.380 --> 00:46:27.820
And so I hope that I brought all of you to a place that you're struggling to deal with...

00:46:30.789 --> 00:46:38.278
The threat of zombies, the abundance of monsters, St. Christopher, dog-headed men, people who don't fit into categories.

00:46:39.130 --> 00:46:45.420
If you feel uneasy and a bit conflicted and confused that I was able to move from zombies to angel in one fell swoop,

00:46:45.460 --> 00:46:50.100
I'm sorry. I have to apologize. That was really the point of my talk.

00:46:52.140 --> 00:46:57.040
That's what fragmenting looks like, that's what a fragmented age looks like:

00:46:57.040 --> 00:47:03.020
the moment when order is not easy to inhabit because the world is filled with exceptions.

00:47:06.820 --> 00:47:14.900
There's something deeply out of place that I am here at a church conference talking about zombies.

00:47:18.060 --> 00:47:20.019
But that's where we are.

00:47:20.019 --> 00:47:22.619
That's where we are in the world.

00:47:23.529 --> 00:47:30.099
If we think we can deal with this age by simply shutting the door of the church we are going to be in serious trouble.

00:47:31.820 --> 00:47:38.229
And if we think we can deal with this age by letting in the flood waters there is going to be serious trouble, as well.

00:47:40.660 --> 00:47:48.920
So now, what do we do? How do we live? How do we love properly in an age of division and chaos?

00:47:51.560 --> 00:47:57.540
First, I think we need to look at the icon of Pentecost with some humility.

00:47:59.020 --> 00:48:05.300
The chaos of the Edge and the End is death.

00:48:06.200 --> 00:48:10.920
But chaos is also an opportunity for renewal.

00:48:12.020 --> 00:48:18.180
This was true for the Sabbath, it is true for Baptism, and it is true for Holy Saturday

00:48:19.340 --> 00:48:22.120
and, for goodness sake, it's true when we sleep.

00:48:24.140 --> 00:48:31.740
Most of us here are here today because the rigid categories which legislate society are breaking down.

00:48:34.400 --> 00:48:41.140
They've been breaking down for about three generations. If I if I were to ask everyone here what their ancestry is,

00:48:42.040 --> 00:48:45.720
how many are immigrants, or children of immigrants

00:48:47.140 --> 00:48:52.780
I mean, we are standing here in a

00:48:53.040 --> 00:48:58.880
in a church which came from Russia and here we are in the south of the United States.

00:48:58.880 --> 00:49:05.120
I am a French-Canadian Protestant who became an Orthodox icon carver.

00:49:05.860 --> 00:49:10.900
We are the image of the opportunity of Pentecost.

00:49:11.080 --> 00:49:15.540
I mean, would this gathering here have even been possible 50 years ago?

00:49:16.460 --> 00:49:19.200
No, there's no way.

00:49:20.200 --> 00:49:25.260
We can be here because, in the breakdown of categories,

00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:31.520
things that usually would not meet can in fact meet.

00:49:32.900 --> 00:49:39.120
And I believe it's not for nothing that Christianity increased in the twilight of the Roman Empire, where the stretched

00:49:39.880 --> 00:49:48.780
multi-ethnic, multicultural vastness was oscillating between fragmentation, civil war, and the iron fist of a Diocletian figure.

00:49:51.100 --> 00:49:56.820
But here's the kicker - here is the side effect, the surprising side effect of our fragmentation

00:49:58.070 --> 00:50:02.769
Right now, as we are watching the western world being propped up as competing

00:50:03.590 --> 00:50:05.480
identities of race and gender,

00:50:05.480 --> 00:50:10.580
you know, and ethnicity and age and, you name it, any identity you can imagine

00:50:10.580 --> 00:50:13.340
and while simultaneously, there's this paradoxical

00:50:13.580 --> 00:50:16.960
insistence that all identities and distinctions have to be dissolved,

00:50:17.680 --> 00:50:25.440
there are those who are not even in the church not in any church who are getting an inkling that, maybe,

00:50:25.800 --> 00:50:32.860
Christianity, considered even at its minimum as a set of presuppositions,

00:50:32.860 --> 00:50:37.780
maybe it was the only glue that ever held the west together.

00:50:38.320 --> 00:50:41.300
That ever kept the balance

00:50:41.500 --> 00:50:44.760
since the conversion of Rome, even as precarious

00:50:44.760 --> 00:50:49.840
and volatile as that unity sometimes was.

00:50:51.100 --> 00:50:54.600
And so I've been surprised to find myself

00:50:55.140 --> 00:50:59.920
facing all these people, these atheists, these people who don't believe in anything

00:50:59.920 --> 00:51:05.040
who are suddenly looking at Christianity and Christian presuppositions.

00:51:07.360 --> 00:51:09.640
But, of course, the same breakdown, let's make no

00:51:10.160 --> 00:51:14.720
Illusion about this, the same breakdown of society which makes this meeting possible,

00:51:14.720 --> 00:51:21.120
which makes some see that Christianity is valuable, is also the breakdown which

00:51:21.120 --> 00:51:23.980
makes the zombie scratch at our door.

00:51:24.320 --> 00:51:27.940
And it seems that every exception, every aberration, every

00:51:27.940 --> 00:51:33.420
proclivity is looking for its moment in the sun, looking not only to exist in the margin,

00:51:33.440 --> 00:51:38.960
but to be given the stamp of full official recognition.

00:51:40.820 --> 00:51:44.580
But in the rising flood, in the world filled with monsters,

00:51:44.580 --> 00:51:47.254
people are looking for meaning,

00:51:47.254 --> 00:51:50.100
people are so hungry for meaning.

00:51:51.640 --> 00:51:59.880
For the past year or so I have been on an adventure, on a crazy adventure that took me completely by surprise.

00:52:02.160 --> 00:52:09.240
I was asked to do some interview with some professor in Toronto on some random subject,

00:52:10.120 --> 00:52:14.880
and because of that, I'm not going to go into the details,  but all of a sudden

00:52:15.160 --> 00:52:23.180
I was being flooded with letters and letters of atheists,

00:52:27.420 --> 00:52:30.620
atheists who are looking around and are seeing

00:52:31.059 --> 00:52:36.478
the fragmentation as well. They're looking around and they're seeing the hemorrhaging of our culture

00:52:36.480 --> 00:52:40.200
and they're trying to ask, "What can we do to stop the bleeding?"

00:52:47.440 --> 00:52:54.900
a long email every day of some recovering atheist,

00:52:55.959 --> 00:52:58.169
who's moving away from their criticism

00:52:58.169 --> 00:53:04.680
you know, of the 13-year-old's version of Christianity, and is discovering the cosmic vision of Christ.

00:53:08.650 --> 00:53:14.009
He's discovering the cosmic vision of his Mother whose womb is vaster than the heavens.

00:53:14.380 --> 00:53:16.739
There's so much to say to those people.

00:53:18.279 --> 00:53:25.919
I mean, we hold in our hearts the tradition of Christ which connects, through a beautiful braid of analogies, the personal,

00:53:26.199 --> 00:53:29.189
the social, the cosmic, and the Transcendent realms.

00:53:32.200 --> 00:53:37.000
I have not tried to convince anybody. I have no desire to convince anybody.

00:53:37.680 --> 00:53:41.440
The only thing that I ever did was to show people

00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:50.700
the beautiful patterns that are in our Tradition, the beautiful connections between Scripture and the icons and the liturgy and the hymns and

00:53:50.860 --> 00:53:54.900
people are so hungry for meaning that they just

00:53:55.420 --> 00:54:00.020
It's like breathing. It's like it's like all the sudden. They've been drowning and they can

00:54:00.700 --> 00:54:05.580
they can take a breath. I'm not saying that all these people are going to become Orthodox. I mean I don't think so.

00:54:07.440 --> 00:54:11.880
But it's a crazy thing I got a letter from a man from 2-3 days ago

00:54:11.880 --> 00:54:14.430
I got a letter from a man in Pakistan.

00:54:14.980 --> 00:54:20.520
a man who was a Muslim and who in university like so many people became an atheist

00:54:21.040 --> 00:54:23.459
and now he writes me, and he says,

00:54:23.980 --> 00:54:28.040
"I think I'm a Christian. I don't know and I don't know what to do

00:54:28.680 --> 00:54:30.920
because I'm in Pakistan."

00:54:37.680 --> 00:54:43.080
Of all the Christians, we do not see the stories of the Bible, the stories of salvation, as just this arbitrary set

00:54:43.140 --> 00:54:45.760
of facts that we must believe in to be saved.

00:54:45.760 --> 00:54:52.340
Of all Christians, we know the organic process of salvation, the transformation of man into God

00:54:53.280 --> 00:54:59.300
through participation in the life of God, is the very manner by which the world exists.

00:54:59.470 --> 00:55:06.179
It's the very thing that links these outward phenomena to consciousness, to Nous, to Logos

00:55:06.460 --> 00:55:09.440
and unites all things towards the Infinite.

00:55:09.720 --> 00:55:11.760
I mean, how awesome is that?

00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:21.080
And in the background we've also kept and we hold, not only the keys to Christianity,

00:55:23.319 --> 00:55:27.239
we also hold the heritage of the ancients. I mean, we're not

00:55:27.880 --> 00:55:31.820
Platonists, but we have carried Plato. We are not

00:55:31.980 --> 00:55:37.940
Aristotelian, but we have carried Aristotle, Alexander, Homer, Virgil, and we could add to that,

00:55:38.470 --> 00:55:42.480
you know, all the useful stories of all people who enter in communion with Christ.

00:55:42.670 --> 00:55:47.369
We keep them alive in the narthex of the church so that they can participate in

00:55:48.360 --> 00:55:54.040
the manner in which it is possible that they participate in the divine life.

00:55:54.600 --> 00:56:02.080
You'll recognize these are images of the Greek sages from a narthex at a monastery in Greece.

00:56:04.840 --> 00:56:07.780
They don't have halos, so it's okay.

00:56:13.580 --> 00:56:20.240
We hold the golden thread that reaches back into the mists of time,

00:56:21.160 --> 00:56:25.620
the same golden thread that is under increasing threat of being cut.

00:56:31.099 --> 00:56:37.119
So I think the chaos we face is a test, like the waters of baptism it will wash off all that is

00:56:37.640 --> 00:56:41.080
superficial, all that which we have not integrated.

00:56:42.020 --> 00:56:48.380
Encountering the outer darkness, being challenged by the outer darkness is an urgent test for Christians to know

00:56:48.400 --> 00:56:54.180
to the extent that it is possible to know in our hearts and our minds, What exactly is this thing about?

00:56:54.860 --> 00:57:00.660
What is the Incarnation? How does it anchor the world? And, of course, there is Mystery.

00:57:01.580 --> 00:57:05.200
But we can tell people why Mystery matters.

00:57:06.200 --> 00:57:12.760
Why some things cannot be described or contained, and why that Mystery is the fount of existence.

00:57:15.820 --> 00:57:23.300
And I think that the answer in how to engage the fragmenting world, is in the story of Saint Christopher.

00:57:25.880 --> 00:57:31.780
But as we travel farther away from the altar, we never compromise its purity.

00:57:32.980 --> 00:57:37.200
As we move to the nave and through the narthex,

00:57:37.360 --> 00:57:41.340
as we move out in a candlelit procession, out into the world,

00:57:41.600 --> 00:57:45.080
our compassion grows as Christ's compassion grew

00:57:45.320 --> 00:57:52.320
facing the adulterous woman. As Christ's compassion grew facing the rich, thieving tax collector, the heretical

00:57:52.520 --> 00:57:55.780
Samaritan woman. All of this we can do without

00:57:55.980 --> 00:58:01.000
compromising the purity of the altar, the rigor of the sacramental life.

00:58:02.260 --> 00:58:07.820
Christ protected the purity of the Holy Place, he cleared the temple of lies and corruption,

00:58:08.450 --> 00:58:11.139
and Christ can hold a whip in his left hand and

00:58:11.990 --> 00:58:13.990
bless with his right hand.

00:58:14.620 --> 00:58:18.200
We need not confuse the altar with the narthex

00:58:18.900 --> 00:58:27.420
and, in fact, we offer that very structure to the world, the very structure of the Church itself as a ladder,

00:58:28.600 --> 00:58:31.620
And we're willing to plant that first bar

00:58:32.260 --> 00:58:35.500
plant it unapologetically into the mud.

00:58:37.760 --> 00:58:41.000
maybe into the very belly of hell,

00:58:42.520 --> 00:58:45.680
but we know that it reaches all the way to the Heaven of Heavens.

00:58:48.360 --> 00:58:50.360
But,

00:58:50.820 --> 00:58:53.360
as we preserve the

00:58:53.460 --> 00:58:56.980
hierarchy of salvation, we must remain cautious.

00:58:56.980 --> 00:59:01.160
We must remain humble enough to notice

00:59:01.880 --> 00:59:03.860
the Holy Fool

00:59:03.869 --> 00:59:05.869
who's hiding in the chaos,

00:59:06.240 --> 00:59:08.960
the exception who reminds us that

00:59:09.140 --> 00:59:11.860
hierarchy is for us, and not for God,

00:59:12.060 --> 00:59:14.420
who is beyond all distinction.

00:59:16.100 --> 00:59:19.500
We need to be humble enough to perceive that sometimes,

00:59:19.800 --> 00:59:25.160
even if it is rarely, by the grace of our Lord, the monster on the Edge of the World

00:59:25.620 --> 00:59:32.120
will be the very vehicle for Christ to be carried farther out than we ever thought possible.

00:59:32.720 --> 00:59:34.720
Thank you.

00:59:37.900 --> 00:59:39.900
(Whistle)

